
# Chained-Toulmin argdown example: JS Mill, On Liberty

```argdown
===
title: Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. II, closing recapitulation
source: Project Gutenberg eBook #34901, lines 2312-2335
granularity:
  level: meso
  floor: "Accept Mill's stated epistemic and psychological observations in the recapitulation as leaf nodes without unpacking his earlier historical examples."
model:
  mode: strict
===

# Main Conclusion

[Free Expression Necessary]: Freedom of opinion and its expression is essential to human mental well-being.
    {basis: "stated", pointer: "para. 1", source: "We have now recognised the necessity to the mental well-being of mankind (on which all their other well-being depends) of freedom of opinion, and freedom of the expression of opinion, on four distinct grounds"}

# Part 1: Epistemic Grounds (When the Silenced Opinion Is True or Partly True)

<Ground 1 - Fallibility>: Silencing an opinion risks suppressing truth by presuming an infallibility no human possesses.

(1) [Silenced Opinion May Be True]: Any opinion forced into silence may, for all we can certainly know, be true.
    {basis: "stated", pointer: "para. 2", source: "if any opinion is compelled to silence, that opinion may, for aught we can certainly know, be true"}
-- Presumed Infallibility Warrant: Suppressing a potentially true view is only rational if the censor cannot be mistaken {uses: [1], basis: "stated", pointer: "para. 2", source: "To deny this is to assume our own infallibility."} --
(2) [Censorship Assumes Infallibility]: Refusing to hear an opinion presumes that our own judgment is infallible.
-- Human Fallibility Warrant: No person, authority, or majority is infallible {uses: [2], basis: "assumed", reason: "Established earlier in ch. II; left implicit in the closing recapitulation"} --
(3) [Blocks Whole Truth]: Silencing an opinion unjustifiably risks suppressing the truth.

<Ground 2 - Partial Truth>: Even when a silenced opinion is mostly false, suppressing it prevents us from completing the partial truth held by orthodoxy.

(1) [Error Holds Partial Truth]: Even a mistaken opinion commonly contains a portion of the truth.
    {basis: "observed", pointer: "para. 3", source: "though the silenced opinion be an error, it may, and very commonly does, contain a portion of truth"}
(2) [Orthodoxy Is Incomplete]: The prevailing opinion on any complex subject is rarely or never the whole truth.
    {basis: "observed", pointer: "para. 3", source: "the general or prevailing opinion on any subject is rarely or never the whole truth"}
-- Adversarial Synthesis Warrant: Missing elements of truth emerge only when opposing opinions collide in open debate {uses: [1, 2], basis: "theory", pointer: "para. 3", source: "it is only by the collision of adverse opinions, that the remainder of the truth has any chance of being supplied"} --
(3) [Blocks Truth Synthesis]: Silencing erroneous opinions removes the only mechanism that can supply the remainder of the truth.

<Epistemic Branch Synthesis>: Whether a dissenting opinion is wholly true or partly erroneous, silencing it deprives humanity of truth.

(1) [Blocks Whole Truth]
(2) [Blocks Truth Synthesis]
-- Exhaustive Truth-Seeking Warrant: A dissenting opinion containing any truth is either wholly true or partly true, so censorship obstructs truth in both cases {uses: [1, 2], basis: "assumed", reason: "Groups Grounds 1 and 2 into the truth-discovery branch of Mill's case-split"} --
(3) [Deprives Humanity of Truth]: Suppressing opinion obstructs humanity's access to truth.

# Part 2: Intellectual and Moral Grounds (Even When Received Opinion Is Already the Whole Truth)

<Ground 3 - Dead Dogma>: Without contestation, even a completely true belief degenerates into an uncomprehended prejudice.

(1) [Uncontested Truth Becomes Prejudice]: Unless a true belief is vigorously and earnestly contested, most believers hold it as a passive prejudice with little comprehension of its rational grounds.
    {basis: "observed", pointer: "para. 4", source: "unless it is suffered to be, and actually is, vigorously and earnestly contested, it will, by most of those who receive it, be held in the manner of a prejudice, with little comprehension or feeling of its rational grounds"}
-- Rational Understanding Warrant: True beliefs must be understood through their rational grounds rather than held as uncomprehended prejudices {uses: [1], basis: "assumed", reason: "Connects holding truth as a prejudice in para. 4 to intellectual harm"} --
(2) [Erodes Rational Comprehension]: Silencing challenges strips believers of any genuine comprehension of why their true beliefs are true.

<Ground 4 - Lost Vitality>: Without contestation, true doctrine loses its meaning and decays into hollow conformity that blocks real conviction.

(1) [Doctrine Loses Vital Effect]: An unchallenged doctrine loses its meaning and its vital influence on character and conduct.
    {basis: "observed", pointer: "para. 4", source: "the meaning of the doctrine itself will be in danger of being lost, or enfeebled, and deprived of its vital effect on the character and conduct"}
-- Hollow Profession Warrant: When a doctrine loses its vital effect, it becomes a dead formal profession that prevents heartfelt conviction from developing through reason or experience {uses: [1], basis: "observed", pointer: "para. 4", source: "the dogma becoming a mere formal profession, inefficacious for good, but cumbering the ground, and preventing the growth of any real and heartfelt conviction, from reason or personal experience"} --
(2) [Kills Moral Vitality]: Suppressing debate reduces living moral conviction to sterile, empty recitation.

<Conviction Branch Synthesis>: Even when orthodoxy is entirely true, silencing dissent corrupts how that truth is understood and lived.

(1) [Erodes Rational Comprehension]
(2) [Kills Moral Vitality]
-- Living Conviction Warrant: Holding a truth properly requires both comprehending its rational grounds and feeling its vital force on conduct {uses: [1, 2], basis: "assumed", reason: "Combines Mill's cognitive (Ground 3) and character/conduct (Ground 4) harms"} --
(3) [Corrupts How Truth Is Held]: Suppressing challenges to true beliefs degrades rational comprehension and moral conviction.

# Part 3: Thesis Convergence

<Mental Well-Being Synthesis>: Because censorship either deprives us of truth or corrupts how we hold truth, free expression is indispensable to mental well-being.

(1) [Deprives Humanity of Truth]
(2) [Corrupts How Truth Is Held]
-- Mental Well-Being Warrant: Human mental well-being depends both on discovering true beliefs and on holding them as rationally understood, living convictions regardless of whether a challenged view turns out to be true or false {uses: [1, 2], basis: "assumed", reason: "Bridges the four grounds (paras. 2-4) back to 'the mental well-being of mankind' in para. 1"} --
(3) [Free Expression Necessary]

```
